          "heading": "Part One - A Baker from Goa: The Pader's World",
          "para": "Lucio Rodrigues opens with the elders of Goa nostalgically reminiscing about the good old Portuguese days, the Portuguese and their famous loaves of bread; the eaters of loaves may have vanished, he says, but the makers are still amongst us - the mixers, the moulders and those who bake the loaves. The baker, called the pader, was part of every household's day. Morning after morning he made his musical entry with the jingling sound of his specially made bamboo staff, one hand supporting the basket of loaves on his head, the other banging the bamboo on the ground, the bread coming warm from the family brick furnace. The children ran to him and longed for the ring-shaped bread bangles they chose with such care, while the maidservant bought the loaves for the house. The baker traditionally wore the kabai, a single-piece long frock reaching down to the knees; in the narrator's own childhood bakers wore a shirt and trousers that were shorter than full-length ones and longer than half-pants. Bread governed every occasion: marriage gifts are meaningless without the sweet bread called bol, cakes and bolinhas are a must for Christmas and other festivals, and sandwiches must be prepared by the lady of the house on the occasion of her daughter's engagement. Baking was indeed a profitable profession - the baker and his family never starved, and their plump, jackfruit-like physique was an open testimony to their prosperity.",
