          "para": "Lokesh Abrol's travelogue introduces Coorg, midway between Mysore and the coastal town of Mangalore in Karnataka, the smallest district of the state, a land of rolling hills inhabited by a proud race of martial men and beautiful women. Coorg is home to evergreen rainforests, spices such as pepper and cardamom, and sprawling coffee estates; the very air breathes of coffee. During the monsoons it pours enough to keep many visitors away, so the best season is September to March, when the rain is over and the weather is perfect. The Coorgi homes have a tradition of hospitality and recount tales of valour of their sons and fathers; the Coorg regiment is one of the most decorated in the Indian Army, and General Cariappa, the first Indian Commander-in-Chief of the Army, was a Coorgi. The river Kaveri, whose water comes from the hills and forests of Coorg, offers the mahaseer, the largest freshwater fish, while kingfishers dive for it and macaques, Malabar squirrels and wild elephants inhabit the rainforests - a paradise for bird-watchers too. The adventurous can enjoy river rafting, canoeing, rock climbing, mountain biking and trekking; the climb to the Brahmagiri hills brings a panoramic view of the entire mist-covered landscape, and Buddhist monks in red, ochre and yellow robes may be met from the nearby Tibetan settlement of Bylakuppe.",
