          "heading": "Themes Across the Three Glimpses",
          "para": "The three pieces are bound together by the idea that India's real wealth lies in its regional variety and in the everyday culture of ordinary people. A Baker from Goa celebrates cultural memory: a humble profession, the pader's, keeps the Portuguese past alive in Goan kitchens through bol, bolinhas and bread bangles. Coorg celebrates nature's gifts - rainforests, spices, coffee and the Kaveri - joined to human valour and hospitality. Tea from Assam binds trade and tradition: a Chinese leaf, carried through legend and empire, becomes the daily ritual of chai tended by garden workers at Dhekiabari. All three pieces honour ordinary professions - the baker, the tea plucker, the planter - as the true custodians of heritage, and all three insist that heritage is lived experience, carried in smells, tastes and habits, not a museum exhibit. Nostalgia in Goa, pride in Coorg and curiosity in Assam are three moods of one affection: love for the small, local worlds that together make India.",
