          "heading": "Mijbil's Nature and Play",
          "para": "Otters are made for water, and Mijbil's delight in the bath soon became the author's entertainment and occasionally his problem. The famous scene is the tap: Maxwell found the otter in the bathroom turning the tap with his paws and splashing in the water, and in time Mij learned to work the tap so well that he could open it himself whenever he wanted to play, making the tap his greatest game. On dry land his energy found other outlets. He invented games of his own with ping-pong balls, marbles and rubber fruit, juggling small objects between his paws like a small circus performer, and he would take away anything that caught his eye and turn it into a toy under his own rules. The author watched these antics with fascination, discovering new sides to the otter's intelligence every day, and realised that with Mij nothing in the house could be assumed safe, still, or boring.",
