      "boardQuestions": [
        { "question": "What was Valli's deepest desire, and how did it grow?", "marks": 1, "type": "short", "probability": "high", "answer": "The most fascinating thing in Valli's world was the bus that travelled between her village and the nearest town, passing through her street each hour. Watching it fill with a new set of passengers each time was a source of unending joy, and gradually a tiny wish crept into her head and grew there: she wanted to ride on that bus, even if only once. That single desire organised her savings, her planning and finally her secret journey." },
        { "question": "How did Valli save money for her first bus journey?", "marks": 2, "type": "short", "probability": "high", "answer": "The fare was thirty paise one way, so Valli needed sixty paise, and she saved it coin by coin with strict self-control. Every temptation was resisted: she would not spend on peppermints, toys and balloons, and she gave up the treat she longed for most of all - a ride on the merry-go-round at the village fair. Her thrift was part of the plan: the money was kept intact for the two fares, which is why she later refused even the conductor's offer of a cold drink in town." },
        { "question": "How did the conductor treat Valli on the bus?", "marks": 2, "type": "short", "probability": "high", "answer": "The conductor was a jolly, teasing man who entered into Valli's pretence of being a grown-up. He addressed her as 'madam' throughout the ride with mock politeness, assured her that there was room for everybody, and joked with her as he would with an adult passenger. When she stood on the seat he joined the elders in telling her to sit, and in town he offered to buy her a cold drink. His teasing delighted Valli rather than offending her, because it let her play the lady she wished to be." },
        { "question": "What did Valli see from the bus, and how did she react?", "marks": 3, "type": "short", "probability": "high", "answer": "From the bus Valli saw the young green fields, the canal with palm trees beside it, the distant mountains and the blue, blue sky, and the sight left her speechless with wonder. She stood up on her seat to take in everything better, which brought scolding from an elderly man who feared she would fall, and advice from an elderly woman whose mouth was red with betel juice. Valli found the woman repulsive and refused to answer personal questions: she did not wish to reveal that she was travelling alone and in secret. Yet nothing could dull her joy; the ride gave her exactly the spectacle she had saved sixty paise to see." },
        { "question": "Describe the incident of the young cow on the outward journey.", "marks": 3, "type": "short", "probability": "medium", "answer": "On the way to town, a young cow came galloping wildly in front of the bus, its tail held high. The driver sounded his horn again and again, but the more he honked the faster the frightened animal ran, so that man and beast seemed to be playing a game of chase. Valli laughed until there were tears in her eyes at the comic sight. The same cow, met again on the return journey lying dead by the roadside, turns the laughter of this scene into the sadness of the next, making it the hinge of the whole story." },
        { "question": "How did the sight of the dead cow change Valli?", "marks": 5, "type": "long", "probability": "high", "answer": "On the return journey the bus passed the same young cow that had made Valli laugh so heartily on the way to town. It now lay dead by the roadside, a lifeless heap, evidently struck down by some fast-moving vehicle. The sight struck Valli like a blow: the cow that had galloped so merrily, that had made her laugh until there were tears in her eyes, was now just a motionless body. Her joy drained away; she no longer wanted to look out of the window, and the rest of the ride passed in sadness. The incident is the story's quiet lesson in life: joy and sorrow, laughter and death, can lie on the same road. In one afternoon an eight-year-old receives her first knowledge of mortality, which is why the ride home feels like the end of childhood's innocence." },
        { "question": "Draw a character sketch of Valli.", "marks": 5, "type": "long", "probability": "high", "answer": "Valli, eight years old, is curiosity itself: with no playmates of her own age she studies the world from her front doorway and graduates from watching the street to riding through it. She is remarkably determined and disciplined - the wish to ride the bus grows for months, and she saves sixty paise by resisting sweets, toys, balloons and the merry-go-round. She is brave and independent: she plans a secret journey, slips out while her mother naps, boards the bus alone and insists she can take care of herself. She is proud and self-respecting, accepting no favours, refusing to answer personal questions and declining the conductor's cold drink because her money is meant only for fares. She is also sensitive: the dead cow saddens her into silence. Mature beyond her eight years, she guards her secret with dignity - a child who has tasted both wonder and sorrow in a single ride." },
        { "question": "Describe Valli's visit to the town.", "marks": 3, "type": "short", "probability": "medium", "answer": "Valli's town visit lasted only as long as the bus's halt. She did not get down: the streets, shops and crowds were watched from her seat, which was the safest and cheapest grandstand. When the conductor offered her a cold drink, she declined, saying she had no money to spare, for her sixty paise had gone on the fares. She drank in the sights of the town in her own way, and then the bus turned back for the village. Her visit thus costs her nothing beyond the fare and shows her discipline: pleasure taken without spending, adventure without risk." },
        { "question": "'Madam Rides the Bus is the story of a child's initiation into experience.' Discuss.", "marks": 5, "type": "long", "probability": "high", "answer": "The story shows a child moving from second-hand knowledge to first-hand experience. Valli's world begins at her front door, watching the street; her knowledge of the outside is a picture built from watching a bus pass hourly. The disciplined saving of sixty paise, the resistance to merry-go-rounds and sweets, and the secret one o'clock trip show initiative and planning rare in a child of eight. On the bus she enjoys the fields, canal, palms and mountains, but also learns social confidence, facing scolding elders and a teasing conductor without yielding her dignity. The dead cow delivers the deepest initiation: the same animal that made her laugh lies dead on the return road, teaching her without words that joy and death travel together. She returns home before her secret is known, answering her mother's chatter with a knowing smile. The ride is thus her first journey into the adult world of money, danger and loss." },
        { "question": "Valli's bus ride was a carefully planned expedition. Explain.", "marks": 6, "type": "long", "probability": "medium", "answer": "Nothing in Valli's adventure is left to chance. First, information: she watches the bus pass hourly, learns that the fare is thirty paise one way and that the trip takes forty-five minutes. Second, finance: she saves sixty paise, exactly two fares, by refusing peppermints, toys, balloons and the ride she most desires, on the merry-go-round. Third, timing: she chooses the one o'clock afternoon bus, calculating to reach town and return by the same bus at two forty-five, all within the span of her mother's afternoon nap. Fourth, execution: she flags the bus down herself, pays her fare, insists on no help, observes her own rules - she does not leave the bus in town, refuses a cold drink that would disturb her budget, and returns home before anyone misses her. The plan's only unplanned element is the dead cow, and even that she absorbs silently. The expedition reveals a mind of order, thrift and quiet courage behind a child's face." }
      ]
