          "heading": "About the Author and the Play",
          "para": "Anton Chekhov (1860-1904) was a Russian writer who excelled in both the short story and drama, and who was a doctor by profession; his observation of human weakness is always precise and always forgiving. The Proposal (also translated as A Marriage Proposal) is a one-act farce - a short play built not on character development but on exaggeration, improbable situations and escalating nonsense, all designed to provoke laughter. Its target is the marriage customs of the landed gentry: landowners with plenty of property, plenty of leisure and precious little sense, who treat marriage as an economic arrangement between neighbouring estates. The entire action is compressed into the drawing-room of Chubukov's country house, with three characters, one visiting suitor and a single evening in which a proposal is made, lost, found and made again - without ever being calmly discussed.",
